In this lesson, we'll look at the tangent function and what makes it tick. First, think of tangent as a ratio: sine over cosine, whenever cosine isn't zero. Geometrically, it's the slope of the line from the origin to a point on the unit circle. Next, where is tangent undefined? It blows up wherever cosine equals zero, which happens at ninety degrees plus any multiple of one-eighty degrees. These spots are vertical asymptotes, and they're a key feature of the tangent graph. Then, sign by quadrant. In the first quadrant, both sine and cosine are positive, so tangent is positive. In the second, sine is positive but cosine is negative, so tangent is negative. In the third, both are negative, so tangent is positive again. And in the fourth, sine is negative while cosine is positive, so tangent is negative. So the quick rule: tangent is positive in quadrants one and three, and negative in quadrants two and four. Now let's see it in action.